I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern quality, and the frozen divalent-tungstate workflow, then identify candidate phases before building and refining a GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the reported fractions limited to modeled crystalline phases and flag any ambiguity.

The metadata points to the Zn–W–O system (ZnO + WO₃, 200 °C), but that chemistry permits unreacted oxides and more than one zinc tungstate structure. I’m treating phase count as an inference from the full profile rather than assuming ZnWO₄ alone.

Peak positions already distinguish the major components: the 23.13/23.60/24.40° triplet and associated lines match monoclinic WO₃, while 31.80/34.58/36.32/47.68/56.65° match wurtzite ZnO. I’m now testing whether the weaker features require ZnWO₄ or are fully accounted for by these two precursor phases.

The dominant WO₃ cell matches the monoclinic COD model 2311041 especially closely; wurtzite ZnO (COD 9004178) accounts for the second peak family. I’m setting up a staged two-phase Rietveld refinement first, because a real third phase should then appear as coherent unmatched reflections rather than as isolated residual noise.

The two-phase refinement reaches Rwp ≈ 3.91% versus a counting-statistics lower bound of ≈ 2.80%. Its provisional crystalline fractions are about 73 wt% WO₃ and 27 wt% ZnO. No obvious ZnWO₄ lines are needed so far; I’m tightening nuisance/profile terms and examining structured residuals before accepting that phase inventory.

A useful framework detail is that generator-side library CIFs were normalized to neutral atoms and a common isotropic Uiso before simulation. The raw COD ZnO file carries anisotropic displacement data, so I’m recreating that documented normalization; this should remove a real intensity-model mismatch rather than merely add fit flexibility.

With the documented CIF normalization plus refined common Uiso and polarization, the two-phase fit reaches Rwp = 2.816%, essentially the Poisson lower bound of 2.799%. A surface-roughness term gives no meaningful improvement and collapses against its bounds, suggesting that effect is absent. I’m doing a final explicit ZnWO₄ inclusion test and checking structural/GPX consistency.

The final cleaned model is now at the counting-statistics limit: Rwp = 2.8156%, Rexp = 2.7987%, GoF = 1.006. The refined fractions are 0.73899 WO₃ and 0.26101 ZnO; the explicit ZnWO₄ trial refined to only ~0.0004 fraction and became unstable, so it is not a supported crystalline phase.

All required artifacts are written. I’m running the same submission-format, CIF/GPX consistency, physical-contact, and full-grid profile checks used by the frozen evaluator before handing them back.

